<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hyder Ali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1720–1782</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Haidar Ali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hyder Ali was a soldier who rose to command the army of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and from 1761 ruled it in place of the Wodeyar kings. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitradurga">Chitradurga</a> and Malabar, invaded Coorg, and built a modern army with French help. He fought the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in the first two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-mysore-wars">Anglo-Mysore Wars</a> and died during the second, succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hyder-ali.jpg" alt="Hyder Ali" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Hyder_Ali,%22_a_steel_engraving_from_the_1790%27s_(with_modern_hand_coloring).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
